  {
    "id": "how",
    "title": "Power changes its costume",
    "labels": [
      "Who compels whom",
      "Convertible privilege",
      "Graded evidence"
    ],
    "lines": [
      "Its real question is architectural: who can compel whom, and who can replace them?",
      "Privilege lives in office, careers, information and status, and each can convert into another.",
      "When winners can rewrite the rules, market reward becomes political power.",
      "Cited findings, possible mechanisms and working proposals stay apart, so you can weigh the evidence."
    ],
    "emotionalPlan": {
      "arc": "from analytic focus through tension to clarity",
      "purpose": "Show the method: architectural questions, convertible privilege, graded evidence.",
      "states": [
        "focused inquiry",
        "sharpening insight",
        "tension",
        "clarity and trust"
      ],
      "intensities": [
        0.5,
        0.6,
        0.75,
        0.4
      ],
      "voiceDirections": [
        "Crisp and curious; slight pause before the two questions.",
        "Even pace through the list, a small stress on 'convert'.",
        "Firmer and slower, a hint of warning.",
        "Release the tension; plain, reassuring precision."
      ]
    },
